Definition 4.1

Let a and b be two vectors in R³ (not R³). 
The cross product (or vectro product) of a and b, denoted a x b, is the vector whose length and direction are given as follows: 

	1) The lenght of a x b is the area of the paralelogram spanned by a and b or zero if either a is parallel to b or if a or b is 0. Alternatively, the following formula holds: 
			||a x b|| = ||a|| ||b||sinΘ, 

	where Θ is the angle between a and b (see Figure 1.51)

	
	2) The direction of a x b is such that a x b is perpendicular to both a and b (when both a and b are nonzero) and is taken so that the ordered triple (a, b, a x b) is a right-handed set of vectors, as shown in Figure 1.52. (If either a or b is 0, or if a is paralle to b, then a x b = 0 from the aformentioned length condition)

By saying that (a, b, a x b) is right-handed, we mean that if you llet the fingers of your right hand curl from a toward b, then your thumb will point in the direction of a x b. 
